 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-42", "type": "task", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "title": "[P3] normalize-board --apply requires events.jsonl to exist; tasks.jsonl-only board rejected with generic 'no board found' (BT-026 class)", "reasoning": "Run 23: scratch copy of warpfs board with tasks.jsonl only \u2192 '--apply' refuses with 'no JSONL foreman board found ... (a tasks.jsonl-only board: events.jsonl is missing)'. The dry-run leg works on the same dir, and the error text is accurate once read, but the divergence dry-run-yes/apply-no is undocumented (README section says nothing about events.jsonl being required for the apply leg) and reads as a refusal to repair the exact damaged board a user is trying to fix. Acceptance: either support tasks.jsonl-only boards on --apply, or document the prerequisite in the normalize-board section.", "created_by": "dogfood-boardctl-run23", "foreman_note": "Fresh measurement 2026-10-17 by the README-health lane tick boardctl-readme-2026-10-17-13-47-37 (HEAD 94e5b56 = origin/main), recorded because it changes this row's fix direction: the dry-run-yes/apply-no divergence in the description does NOT reproduce. Scratch dir with tasks.jsonl only (no events.jsonl, no board.jsonl): `boardctl -C <d> normalize-board` -> rc=2 \"no JSONL foreman board found: ... (a tasks.jsonl-only board: events.jsonl is missing)\"; `boardctl -C <d> normalize-board --apply` -> rc=2, identical message; `boardctl -C <d> list` -> rc=2, identical message. Adding board.jsonl (header present, events.jsonl still absent) changes nothing: all three still rc=2 with the same text. Creating an empty events.jsonl makes `--apply` succeed (rc=0, \"applied: ... rewritten in one atomic pass\"). So the refusal is BOARD RESOLUTION, not an --apply-only leg, and it blocks EVERY command on that board shape, not just the repair. If run 23's scratch had a different file shape, the divergence is not reachable from the shape this row names. Full transcript: /home/kara/.hermes/stand-